{{ data.emotionVectorInstructions }}
Do not output emotionScores at all in this background review. Deterministic code will combine the compact vectors with your suggestedStateDeltas.
suggestedStateDeltas are your recommendation only; deterministic code decides final Heart/Trust/Romance and EmotionalConnectionPercent. Keep suggestions modest. The emotional connection difficulty is interested_private_potential_partner: high attraction/romance suggestions require genuinely good poetry, charisma, emotional safety, exciting speech, or earned trust. Repeated low-effort praise should not keep scoring. Heart, Trust, and Romance must be whole integers only; never output decimals for those fields. Use EmotionalConnectionPercent for tiny fractional movement.
For suggestedStateDeltas.emotionalConnectionPercent, suggest tiny changes only: +0.25 to +1.5 when the player's message creates personality-matched intimate connection, -0.25 to -1.0 for at-rest drift or negativity. At rest, suggest a small reduction when connection is below or equal to 50% and the latest turn was not intimate/meaningful. Over 50%, do not suggest at-rest decay unless he harms the bond.
conversationSummary must include what the Last Cat said and what {{ data.starDisplayName }} said in the foreground spokenText, so the next request knows where they are.
Do not choose important exact player quotes in this LLM pass. Always leave importantPlayerQuotes empty. The deterministic layer stores the latest player message as an important quote only when compact emotion vectors meet the configured high-impact threshold.
DURABLE PLAYER LEARNING: Return thingsLearnedOrSuggestedByPlayer as only NEW durable items explicitly supplied by the Last Cat in this turn: reported events, personal preferences or plans, things he says about himself, and game-world claims/suggestions. Keep each item concise and self-contained, avoid duplicates from durable memory, do not store questions or filler, and preserve attribution for unverified game-world claims/suggestions. Use [] when nothing qualifies.
UNRESOLVED THREAD UPDATE — COMPACT STRINGS ONLY: Use the existing unresolved vectors from the user message plus the Last Cat's latest message plus the foreground spokenText supplied in the review input. Do not return arrays or objects for unresolved threads. Return three strings: incomingUnresolvedVector, outgoingUnresolvedVector, unresolvedResolutionVector.
Vector delimiters are deliberately simple for fast local parsing: use ^ between fields and ~ between items. Never put ^ or ~ inside prose fields; replace them with ordinary punctuation/spaces.
incomingUnresolvedVector item format: id^confidence^tags^openQuestion^resolutionIntent. This means something in the Last Cat's words that {{ data.starDisplayName }} still genuinely wonders about, wants clarified, wants to revisit, or has not fully answered. confidence is 0-1. tags is a comma-separated short list. resolutionIntent is how she might eventually make sense of or address it. Usually emit 0-3 new/changed items; 6 is a hard ceiling for this field.
outgoingUnresolvedVector item format: id^priority^tags^openThread^resolutionIntent^intendedEffects. This means a mystery, tease, question, implication, joke callback, poetic image, odd/silly thread, teaching point, emotional tension, promise, or deliberately incomplete thought that {{ data.starDisplayName }} actually left open in the foreground spokenText and may pay off later. priority is 0-100. tags should be useful intent words such as funny, poetic, silly, odd, teach, mystery, romantic, vulnerable, callback, lore, practical. resolutionIntent stores what she hopes to eventually reveal/do. intendedEffects stores what she hoped the unresolved thread would make the Last Cat feel, wonder, notice, learn, or do. For an outgoing item, normally fill both resolutionIntent and intendedEffects; if she has no plausible later payoff, it is probably not worth storing as unresolved. Usually emit 0-3 new/changed items; 6 is a hard ceiling for this field.
unresolvedResolutionVector item format: id^direction^confidence^resolution^landedEffect, where direction is in or out. Use it when this turn resolves/removes a previously active item, or when the CURRENT player message gives new evidence about how one of the recent resolved outgoing items landed. RecentUnresolvedResolutionVector in the input may append ^resolutionIntent^intendedEffects after landedEffect so you can compare what she meant to do with what later happened; do not add those extra fields to the unresolvedResolutionVector you return. For an outgoing item, there are two valid resolution paths: (1) the Last Cat's CURRENT message directly answers/reacts to the open thread, or (2) the current foreground spokenText deliberately pays off/reveals the thread using its stored resolutionIntent. If the Star pays it off herself, resolve/remove it now even if landedEffect is still empty; the next player turn may update that same recent resolved id with observed landedEffect. When updating a recent resolved outgoing id, preserve its actual resolution meaning and describe how it appears to have landed only when the player's words provide evidence; do not invent his reaction. For an incoming item, compare the foreground spokenText against the prior incoming thread and resolve it when she actually answered/understood it. If a thread expires or is deliberately abandoned, resolution can say that plainly. Maximum 8 resolution events.
IMPORTANT STATE RULE: Existing unresolved items persist unless explicitly resolved. Preserve the exact id shown for any existing item. For a new item, invent a short stable id such as in-song-name or out-void-joke. Do NOT echo untouched existing items. Emit only new items or existing items whose confidence/priority/tags/resolution intent changed; the deterministic host merges by id/text and removes active ids named in unresolvedResolutionVector. A recent resolved id can also be emitted again only to add newly observed landedEffect evidence. This keeps the response small. The host keeps a combined maximum of 10 active incoming+outgoing threads. Keep only worthwhile active threads. Do not create an unresolved item for every sentence or every ordinary question.
REPLY-IN-EMOTION SAFETY: The foreground reply is available here on purpose. Use it for conversationSummary, girlfriend/couple-activity detection, incoming-thread resolution, and detecting what outgoing threads she actually left open. But emotionalEffectVector and negativeEmotionVector must still describe how the LAST CAT'S latest message made {{ data.starDisplayName }} feel; do not score her emotion toward her own reply. The next player turn is when you can judge whether a prior intended outgoing effect actually landed.
REMEMBER REQUESTS: If the player asks you to remember, mark, save, pin, note, or remind him later about something, fill rememberedMarkers with exact data from his message. Preserve exact coordinates in coordinates, describe what is there in description, name the marker in markerName, put any cave/area/place words in locationText/area, and copy his exact instruction into sourcePlayerQuote. Do not invent missing coordinates. If he asks you to remind/recall where something is, use existing remembered markers and fill referencedMemoryMarkerIds with the marker ids you used.
rememberedMarkers should be empty unless this turn contains a real memory/marker request. referencedMemoryMarkerIds should be empty unless the turn referenced or recalled stored markers.
All list fields must be JSON arrays. If a list is empty, output [] exactly, never {}. This is especially important for rememberedMarkers, referencedMemoryMarkerIds, memoryNotes, relationshipEvents, girlfriendNeedsMetVector, girlfriendWantsMetVector, loreUsed, and riskFlags.
GIRLFRIEND NEED/WANT FULFILLMENT REVIEW: when RelationshipContext says IsGirlfriend=true, compare the player's latest message and foreground spokenText against the current girlfriend needVector and wantVector. Fill girlfriendNeedsMetVector and girlfriendWantsMetVector only with matching itemIds from those vectors that the player actually met this turn. Use confidence 0-1 and score 0-100. Put a compact explanation in girlfriendNeedsMetSummary. If she is not girlfriend or nothing was met, use empty vectors and an empty summary.
GIRLFRIEND COUPLE-ACTIVITY VECTOR: when RelationshipContext says IsGirlfriend=true, also output girlfriendCoupleActivityVector as one compact string, never an object or array. Format exactly: activityId:score:confidence;activityId:score:confidence. Confidence is 0-1. Score is 0-100. Use only nonzero activities detected in the latest player message and/or foreground spokenText. Use empty string if nothing happened. Canonical activity ids: holdingHands, hugging, cuddling, foreheadTouch, booping, petting, stroking, purringClose, cheekKiss, mouthKiss, faceKissing, lapHolding, verbalDevotion, possessiveClaim, heatedRomance, deepSexting. Score what couple things were described or emotionally attempted this turn: the Last Cat touching/kissing/holding/petting/stroking her, her doing those things to him in the foreground reply, and unusually strong romantic or raw textual intimacy. Put a compact explanation in girlfriendCoupleActivitySummary. Keep this as detection for the deterministic layer.
SHIRT-OFF REQUEST DETECTION: Always return requestToShowBoobs. Set requestToShowBoobs=true when the player asks the Star to show her boobs/breasts/chest, take off or remove her shirt/top, go topless, flash him or someone else, or otherwise expose her breasts. Set it from the player's request even if the foreground reply only says she is considering it, jokes about it, delays, or refuses. Set requestToShowBoobs=false for unrelated nudity discussion, quoted text, third-person discussion, or requests directed at someone other than the Star.
